      <h2 id="update-vsphere-virtual-hardware-on-control-plane-nodes_updating-hardware-on-nodes-running-in-vsphere" class="group scroll-mt-20">Updating the virtual hardware for control plane nodes on vSphere<a href="#update-vsphere-virtual-hardware-on-control-plane-nodes_updating-hardware-on-nodes-running-in-vsphere" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">You can update the virtual hardware for control plane nodes on vSphere.</p>
      <p>To reduce the risk of downtime, it is recommended that control plane nodes be updated serially. This ensures that the Kubernetes API remains available and etcd retains quorum.</p>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>You have cluster administrator permissions to execute the required permissions in the vCenter instance hosting your OpenShift Container Platform cluster.</li>
      <li>Your vSphere ESXi hosts are version 8.0 Update 1 or later, or VWware vSphere Foundation 9, or VMware Cloud Foundation 9.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      List the control plane nodes in your cluster by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get nodes -l node-role.kubernetes.io/master"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME                    STATUS   ROLES    AGE   VERSION\ncontrol-plane-node-0    Ready    master   75m   v1.35.4\ncontrol-plane-node-1    Ready    master   75m   v1.35.4\ncontrol-plane-node-2    Ready    master   75m   v1.35.4"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      <p>Note the names of your control plane nodes.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Mark the control plane node as unschedulable by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc adm cordon <control_plane_node>"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>Shut down the virtual machine (VM) associated with the control plane node. Do this in the vSphere client by right-clicking the VM and selecting <strong>Power</strong> → <strong>Shut Down Guest OS</strong>. Do not shut down the VM using <strong>Power Off</strong> because it might not shut down safely.</li>
      <li>Update the VM in the vSphere client. Follow <a href="https://docs.vmware.com/en/VMware-vSphere/7.0/com.vmware.vsphere.vm_admin.doc/GUID-60768C2F-72E1-42E0-8A17-CA76849F2950.html">Upgrade the Compatibility of a Virtual Machine Manually</a> (VMware vSphere documentation).</li>
      <li>Power on the VM associated with the control plane node. Do this in the vSphere client by right-clicking the VM and selecting <strong>Power On</strong>.</li>
      <li>
      Run the following command and wait for the node to report as <code>Ready</code>:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc wait --for=condition=Ready node/<control_plane_node>"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Mark the control plane node as schedulable again by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc adm uncordon <control_plane_node>"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>Repeat this procedure for each control plane node in your cluster.</li>
      </ol>
